menu "Raw touch frames"

config ZMK_RAW_TOUCH
    bool "Stream raw absolute touch frames over a vendor HID report"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_PAD_ENABLED
    depends on ZMK_POINTING
    # The feature report publishes the SoC's hwinfo device id, so a host
    # can recognize one keyboard across USB and Bluetooth. Without a
    # HWINFO driver the field is simply reported as unknown, but the call
    # has to link.
    select HWINFO
    help
      Stream absolute touch frames (position + touch strength) from a
      trackpad running in absolute mode to the host over a vendor-defined
      HID report on its own HID interface / HIDS instance, so no fork of
      ZMK core is required.

      Relative deltas derived from successive frames are always re-injected
      into the pad's normal input listener chain as well, so ordinary
      pointing (and any keymap wheel-mapping overlays) keep working with
      no host software at all (Standard mode). Frames are only emitted
      while a host holds a lease on them (RawTouch mode).

      Configure pads with zmk,raw-touch-pad devicetree nodes.

if ZMK_RAW_TOUCH

config ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_USB
    bool "Send raw touch frames over USB"
    default y
    depends on ZMK_USB
    # Only the central half talks to the host. The core ZMK_RAW_TOUCH symbol
    # needs no such dependency: the pad nodes that enable it belong on the
    # central, and a build with no transport at all still links against a
    # no-op send (src/raw_touch_endpoints.c) and keeps Standard mode.
    depends on !ZMK_SPLIT || ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    help
      Registers a SECOND USB HID interface (HID_1) carrying only the raw
      touch report. Requires CONFIG_USB_HID_DEVICE_COUNT >= 2.

config ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_USB_QUEUE_SIZE
    int "Max number of raw touch reports to queue for sending over USB"
    default 4
    range 2 255
    depends on ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_USB
    help
      Frames waiting for the interrupt IN endpoint.

      The endpoint carries one transfer at a time and the queue is drained
      by transfer completion, so it only has to cover what can pile up
      within one host poll interval: the other pad sampling at the same
      moment, or a peripheral pad's frames arriving from the split link in
      a burst. 4 covers both with room to spare. A deeper queue would only
      add latency - a frame the host receives late is worth less than the
      fresh one behind it.

      When the queue is full the oldest MOTION frame is evicted; release
      frames (touched = 0) are never evicted, because losing one leaves the
      host holding a finger-down until its silence watchdog fires. If no
      entry can be evicted, the incoming frame is dropped - even a release
      - and the log warns that the queue is full of undelivered release
      frames. Raise this if you see that warning: it means more pads than
      the queue can hold.

config ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_BLE
    bool "Send raw touch frames over BLE"
    default y
    depends on ZMK_BLE
    depends on !ZMK_SPLIT || ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    help
      Registers a SECOND HID-over-GATT (HIDS) service instance carrying
      only the raw touch report.

      NOTE: host support for two HIDS instances on one peripheral is not
      universal. If your host ignores the second service, disable this and
      use USB for the raw frames; Standard mode still works over BLE.

config ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_BLE_QUEUE_SIZE
    int "Max number of raw touch reports to queue for sending over BLE"
    default 8
    range 2 255
    depends on ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_BLE
    help
      Frames waiting for a BLE connection event.

      Sized to cover arrival batching, not to buffer a backlog. Frames are
      ~10 ms apart and a host collects them in bursts about one connection
      interval wide (~15 ms on macOS), so 8 frames is ~80 ms of slack -
      several intervals' worth. A frame older than that is useless for
      scrolling: the host reconstructs motion from the device timestamps in
      the frames it does get, and a stale position only delays the fresh
      one behind it.

      When the queue is full the oldest MOTION frame is evicted; release
      frames (touched = 0) are never evicted and are retried on a transient
      notify error. If no entry can be evicted, the incoming frame is
      dropped - even a release, which leaves the host holding a
      finger-down until its silence watchdog fires - and the log warns
      that the queue is full of undelivered release frames. A smaller
      queue makes that more likely. Raise this if you see that warning: it
      means more pads than the queue can hold.

config ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_BLE_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
    int "Stack size of the BLE send work queue"
    default ZMK_BLE_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
    depends on ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_BLE
    help
      The module runs its own work queue for BLE notifications so that a
      frame burst can never delay ZMK's own HID reports.

endif # ZMK_RAW_TOUCH

config ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_RAW_TOUCH_SCROLL
    bool "Raw touch scroll-context marker input processor"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_RAW_TOUCH_SCROLL_ENABLED
    depends on ZMK_POINTING
    help
      A pass-through processor whose presence in the chain handling a
      pad's events marks those frames as scroll context (frame flags
      bit 1). Place it first in the chain.

config ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_RAW_TOUCH_IDLE_FILTER
    bool "Raw touch idle-sync filter input processor"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_RAW_TOUCH_IDLE_FILTER_ENABLED
    depends on ZMK_POINTING
    help
      Drops sync events that accumulated no host-visible change, so an
      absolute-mode pad streaming at ~100 Hz does not also emit ~100 Hz
      all-zero mouse HID reports (which on BLE compete with the raw
      frames for connection events).

config ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_RAW_TOUCH_SPLIT_STAMP
    bool "Peripheral sample-time stamp for relayed raw touch pads"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_RAW_TOUCH_SPLIT_STAMP_ENABLED
    # Peripheral-only: it reports through the split link's peripheral API,
    # which does not exist on the central. The central side needs nothing
    # enabled - raw_touch.c always understands the stamp.
    depends on ZMK_INPUT_SPLIT && !ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    help
      Chained on a split peripheral's zmk,input-split relay, sends the
      peripheral's own sample time along with every absolute frame, so a
      relayed pad's frame timestamps stop carrying the split link's
      delivery jitter (up to one poll cycle on a polled wired link).

module = ZMK_RAW_TOUCH
module-str = ZMK_RAW_TOUCH
source "${ZEPHYR_BASE}/subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endmenu

# The raw touch report needs a second USB HID interface alongside ZMK's.
# Module Kconfig is sourced before Zephyr's subsys/, so this default takes
# precedence over the subsystem's own default of 1; an application .conf
# that pins the symbol lower still wins, which raw_touch_usb_hid.c
# BUILD_ASSERTs against.
config USB_HID_DEVICE_COUNT
    default 2 if ZMK_RAW_TOUCH_USB
